WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.297 --> 00:00:05.652
[PIANO MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:09.520
HOST: Welcome to The Random Fish.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:13.240
We hope you have a continuously lovely evening.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:15.010
Here is our menu.

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:16.870
NORMA: What do you recommend?

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:20.980
HOST: Each of our dishes has its own special flavor and story.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:24.260
Our most popular fish is the Normal fish.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:29.230
But if you'd like something more exotic, you can try the Cauchy fish.

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:32.470
It has an amazingly thick, juicy tail.

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:33.960
Or the Expo fish.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.410
It will make your hunger decay exponentially.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:42.640
And now for tonight's entertainment, this is our star, Randi.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.360
She loves swimming all around her tank randomly.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:50.120
Your challenge, if you choose to accept it,

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:53.440
is to predict her horizontal position in the tank

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.170
and answer questions about Randi's probabilities.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:59.590
JOE BLITZSTEIN: Randi and her tank are two dimensional.

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:02.410
The area of the water is 1.

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:07.480
Each time Randi pauses, her position is uniformly random in the tank.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:11.050
This means the probability of her being in a particular region

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:13.700
is the area of that region.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:17.710
For example, the probability that Randi is in this triangle

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:22.210
is half the probability that she is in this square, whose area is double

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.720
the triangle's area.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.700
And the probability of being in the square

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:32.400
is the same as the probability of being in this blob with the same area.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:33.900
HOST: OK everyone.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:38.790
What's the probability that Randi is at x equals 1.5?

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:41.910
We are looking at her horizontal position in the tank

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:44.820
based on where her eye is.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:45.810
BERNIE: That's easy.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:49.350
I'll just measure the height of the tank at x equals 1.5.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.270
OK-- 0.33.

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.545
NORMA: No, the probability is 0.

00:01:53.545 --> 00:01:55.020
BERNIE: Wait, that's crazy.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.960
Doesn't that mean we would never see Randi at 1.5?

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.590
Look, she's there right now.

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:04.380
NORMA: Randi is approximately at 1.5, but do you really think it's exact?

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:06.870
1.50000...

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.009
forever?

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:10.176
BERNIE: Well, she's at 1.5 as far as we can tell.

00:02:10.176 --> 00:02:13.050
Even with the best ruler in the world, there's a limit to how precise

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:14.580
our measurements can be.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:18.810
NORMA: So when you say the probability of being at 1.5 is 0.33,

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:25.440
do you mean the probability of being at 1.50, 1.500, or what?

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:26.640
BERNIE: Um, I don't know.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.430
NORMA: Randi's position is a continuous random variable,

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:32.160
even if we can't measure it to infinite precision.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:38.322
The probability is 0 that her position is 1.50000 forever.

00:02:38.322 --> 00:02:40.530
BERNIE: I know that's what's taught in Continuopolis,

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:44.330
but it doesn't seem very practical just to say the probability is 0.

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:46.830
NORMA: Can we talk about something other than fish?

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:48.780
I'm just not sure we're compatible.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:51.510
I admire how you handle challenges so straightforwardly

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:55.290
and with such discretion, but we live in such different spaces.

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:56.880
BERNIE: Norma, I love you.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.010
I know I'm not the smoothest guy in the world,

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:00.840
but we actually have a lot in common.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:04.050
And together, combining discrete and continuous perspectives,

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:06.900
we can achieve so much more than we could separately.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:08.310
NORMA: I love you, too.

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:10.890
We do speak the same language at least, the language

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.910
of randomness and uncertainty.

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:15.550
Let's continue trying to understand each other then.

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:16.050
BERNIE: OK.

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:19.050
I agree that Randi's position is continuous, not discrete.

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:21.030
She's a fish swimming in the water, not a frog

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:23.950
jumping from one lily pad to another.

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:27.540
But then how can the probability of being at 1.5 be 0?

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:31.272
Doesn't that mean it's impossible for Randi to be at 1.5?

00:03:31.272 --> 00:03:32.980
But to get from the left half of the tank

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:36.010
to the right half of the tank she must pass through 1.5.

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:37.270
NORMA: Good point.

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:40.510
To swim from x equals 1 to x equals 2, Randi

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:43.700
must cross through every real number from 1 to 2.

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:46.060
Ancient Statland philosophers were puzzled

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:48.260
about how that could be possible.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:51.280
How can Randi pass by infinitely many intermediate points

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:53.580
in a finite amount of time?

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:57.530
Anyway, if we observe Randi over a long time interval,

00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:02.230
it is very likely that Randi will visit x equals 1.5 at least once.

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:05.900
But here, we are checking on Randi at one time point.

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:08.170
BERNIE: So the probability is 0 that Randi is exactly

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:10.540
at 1.5 at that one time point.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:15.220
But as a practical matter, what's the probability that Randi is close to 1.5?

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:18.700
NORMA: Well, then we should specify an error tolerance.

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:20.200
How close is close?

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:26.410
If say, we want Randi to be within 0.05 of 1.5 we can look at the probability

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:30.520
that she is between 1.45 and 1.55.

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:32.530
BERNIE: And how would you compute that?

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:37.757
NORMA: Just find the area under the curve from 1.45 to 1.55.

00:04:37.757 --> 00:04:39.590
JOE BLITZSTEIN: The curve formed by the tank

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:43.600
is a probability density function, also known as a PDF

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:47.170
(not to be confused with Portable Document Format.)

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:53.200
A PDF is a non-negative function such that the area under the curve is 1.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:56.710
For a continuous random variable x, the probability

00:04:56.710 --> 00:05:00.130
that x is in a particular interval is the area

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:03.790
under the PDF curve over that interval.

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:07.270
Note that the PDF of Randi's horizontal position

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:11.770
is the curve formed by the tank since the probability of her being

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:15.300
in a region is the area of that region.

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:17.030
HOST: Well, that's great.

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:19.310
Now time for a new question.

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:25.010
What's the probability that Randi is between x equals 1 and x equals 2?

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:26.210
NORMA: Same idea.

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:30.560
We just need the area under the curve from x equals 1 to x equals 2.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:32.990
Seems messy to compute though.

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:34.990
BERNIE: But wait, there's a sweet symmetry here.

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:39.770
Randi is equally likely to be in the intervals 0 to 1, 1 to 2, and 2 to 3.

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:43.817
So by symmetry, the probably is one third of being between 1 and 2.

00:05:43.817 --> 00:05:44.650
NORMA: You're right.

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:45.730
That's beautiful.

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:48.910
I didn't realize that breaking the problem up into discrete chunks

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:50.200
would help so much.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:51.850
HOST: Well done, everyone.

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:56.710
Now what's the probability that Randi is between 1.5 and 2?

00:05:56.710 --> 00:06:00.460
NORMA: We need the area under the curve from 1.5 to 2.

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:05.122
We can't invoke symmetry here, so finding this area requires calculus.

00:06:05.122 --> 00:06:08.080
BERNIE: As an approximation though, how about making a grid in the tank

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:10.120
and using a discrete random variable?

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:10.990
NORMA: Good idea.

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:13.810
That's like how we can approximate the area of a region

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:18.100
by adding up the areas of lots of narrow rectangles.

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:20.410
IN UNISON: 0.14.

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:24.400
HOST: Our final round for the evening will be a face-off between the two

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:27.040
tables that have done the best so far.

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:31.300
Norma and Bernie and Gamma and Gumbel.

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:35.530
Instead of having a probability for Randi, let's up the ante

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:37.390
and give a prediction.

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:40.240
Where do you think she is?

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:43.450
The table with the answer that's closer to the truth

00:06:43.450 --> 00:06:47.950
will win a free trip to the Statland Games in Averagemont.

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:49.840
BERNIE: What if there's a tie?

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:53.770
HOST: Ha, well, that may be common where you come from,

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:57.910
but here in Continuopolis we don't worry about ties.

00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:02.890
The probability is 0 that two independent continuous random variables

00:07:02.890 --> 00:07:03.642
will be equal.

00:07:03.642 --> 00:07:05.350
BERNIE: But I'm not sure the guesses will

00:07:05.350 --> 00:07:07.420
be specified to infinite precision.

00:07:07.420 --> 00:07:09.460
And two tables could follow the same strategy

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:11.200
and arrive at exactly the same guess.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:15.831
HOST: Well, in that unlikely event I will choose the winner randomly.

00:07:15.831 --> 00:07:16.330
BERNIE: OK.

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:19.370
So half the time Randi is to the left of 1.42

00:07:19.370 --> 00:07:22.510
and half the time she is to the right of 1.42.

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:24.610
So how about guessing 1.42?

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:26.080
NORMA: That's the median.

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:27.280
But here is the mean--

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:28.810
the center of mass.

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:31.202
So how about guessing 1.46?

00:07:31.202 --> 00:07:33.160
BERNIE: How can we decide which form of average

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:35.860
to use for our prediction, the mean or the median?

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:38.350
NORMA: Last time I visited Averagemont I learned

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:42.220
that if we are predicting a quantity x and want to minimize the expected

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:47.150
absolute difference between x and our prediction, we should guess the median.

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:50.500
But if we want to minimize the expected squared difference between x

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:53.260
and our prediction, we should guess the mean.

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:55.870
BERNIE: But the closest guess in terms of absolute difference

00:07:55.870 --> 00:07:59.020
will also be the closest guess in terms of squared difference.

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:02.780
So it's still not clear whether we should choose the mean or the median.

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:03.810
NORMA: A-ha.

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:07.270
Let's keep in mind that we are trying to do better than the other table,

00:08:07.270 --> 00:08:10.210
not trying to optimize some measure of long-run performance

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:12.550
if we play this game many times.

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:16.510
If we guess the median and the other table guesses a different value, C,

00:08:16.510 --> 00:08:20.260
we have more than a 50% chance that our guess will be closer.

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:23.380
If C is greater than the median, we win for all the cases

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:25.690
to the left of the median plus everything

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:27.970
up to halfway between the guesses.

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:32.049
Similarly, if C is less than the median, we win for all cases

00:08:32.049 --> 00:08:36.220
to the right of the median plus everything up to halfway between.

00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:36.970
BERNIE: Beautiful.

00:08:36.970 --> 00:08:38.053
So lets guess the median--

00:08:38.053 --> 00:08:41.130
1.42.

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:44.770
HOST: There's Randi at 1.37.

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:51.130
Norma and Bernie guessed 1.42, while Gamma and Gumbel guessed 1.46.

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:53.890
Congratulations, Norma and Bernie.

00:08:53.890 --> 00:08:59.650
Your outstanding thinking about averages has earned you a free trip

00:08:59.650 --> 00:09:02.504
to the Statland Games in Averagemont!

00:09:02.504 --> 00:09:04.420
JOE BLITZSTEIN: Bernie and Norma have realized

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:07.810
that discrete and continuous thinking are complementary.

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:11.680
Each can give useful approximations and insights for the other.

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:15.970
Together, discrete and continuous approaches form a powerful framework

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:17.050
for modeling the world.

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:20.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

